<h2>The question</h2>

<p><a href="/backlog/3732-decision-where-backlog-ids-are-assigned-so-a-temporary-hash/">#3732</a> asks where a
backlog card's sequential number (NNN) is assigned, so that a card still carrying its temporary hash id can never
reach <code>main</code>. Cards are born with a hash id in a lane branch and renumbered later
(<a href="/backlog/2288-jit-backlog-numbering-drain-assigns-the-nnn-at-land-from-a-h/">#2288</a>). Today the drain publishes the merge first and numbers afterwards,
so any failure in between leaves a hash on <code>main</code>. It happened twice on 2026-09-19: card 3707, then nine
cards (3708–3716) across four pull requests, all merged by the drain. The full working is in
<a href="/reports/2026-09-21-backlog-id-assignment-prior-art.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>Measured on 2026-09-21</h2>

<ul>
  <li>The repo is public and user-owned, with no organization. Merge commit, squash and rebase are all allowed.
    Main requires <code>test</code> and <code>smoke</code>, both pinned to the GitHub Actions app; strict mode is
    off; <code>enforce_admins</code> is off; there are no rulesets.</li>
  <li>The drain merges with <code>--merge</code> inside its land mutex and numbers afterwards
    (<code>scripts/merge-ai-prs.mjs</code>, the <code>mergePr</code> call, then <code>numberPendingHashes</code>).
    It also pushes to <code>main</code> directly for regen, unqueue and resolve-on-land commits.</li>
  <li>The drain already rebuilds a pull request's tip on top of <code>main</code> with plumbing before it merges
    (<code>rebaseDropManifest</code>, #2198). Numbering a tip before merge is the same move with a different tree
    edit.</li>
  <li>GitHub's PR counter is at 2422. Backlog ids already run to 3836, so GitHub numbers overlap existing ids and
    cannot be used as ids.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Finding 1: two invariants hide inside the card</h2>

<p>"No hash-named file in the candidate tree" is per-PR and compositional: a clean <code>main</code> plus pull
requests that each add no hash file stays clean, so a required check on the candidate tree closes it. "NNNs are
unique" is not compositional: two PRs that each pick 3900 merge with no textual conflict, so it needs one
allocation authority. The original draft mixed them, and its first two forks were the same choice under two names.</p>

<h2>Finding 2: prior art does not support a long-lived staging branch</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Gating systems test an ephemeral candidate.</strong> bors and homu (a per-batch <code>staging</code>
    branch fast-forwarded on green), Zuul (speculative future states), Kubernetes Tide (retests against the newest
    base), Chromium's commit queue, and Mergify (temporary branches and draft PRs) all test a candidate and then
    advance or discard it. None promotes the trunk from a long-lived accumulating branch. A discarded candidate
    needs no repair policy; a shared branch does (revert or reset, stacked-PR replay).</li>
  <li><strong>No mature system renumbers after merge.</strong> Gerrit, Phabricator, GitHub, Rust RFCs (PR number)
    and Kubernetes KEPs (issue number) allocate early from an atomic counter. Changesets, Alembic, Rails and
    log4brains never assign a sequence. Python PEPs use human editors as the single writer.</li>
  <li><strong>A git ref is a proven atomic counter.</strong> Gerrit's <code>RepoSequence</code> keeps the counter as
    a blob under <code>refs/sequences/*</code>; the git push protocol carries <code>old-id</code> per ref update and
    the server rejects the update if the ref moved. Whether GitHub honours this on a custom ref under 24 concurrent
    lanes was not tested.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Finding 3: what GitHub can and cannot enforce on this repo</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Native merge queue: unavailable.</strong> GitHub's own gating text limits it to public repositories
    owned by an organization (or Enterprise Cloud). A queue also has no documented way for a check to rewrite the
    queued commit, so it cannot number.</li>
  <li><strong>Push rulesets (restrict file paths): unavailable.</strong> They exist only for private or internal
    repositories. A path rule against hash-named files is not possible; a required status check is.</li>
  <li><strong>Available:</strong> required checks, <code>enforce_admins</code>, squash-only through repository
    settings, and branch rulesets (free on public repos). Branch-protection "restrict who can push" and bypass
    lists are organization features.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipped jobs pass.</strong> A required job skipped by an <code>if:</code> reports success, so the hash
    check must have no <code>if:</code> and no path filter.</li>
  <li><strong>Squash-only closes reachability.</strong> A squash merge puts one commit on <code>main</code>, so the
    lane's commits (each of which added the hash-named file) are not ancestors of it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this reshapes</h2>

<p>The item's original default was a persistent integration branch with one serialized numbering writer. Its four
forks are now two, on the two invariants. Fork 1 is where numbering happens: the producer numbers at PR open, before CI and review, as an operation callable on any lane ref (the conveyor fix agents push without <code>pr-land</code>), and the drain refuses a clashing NNN at the point main is written. An atomic git-ref counter as allocator is rejected on evidence: the write-point check already guarantees uniqueness. Fork 2 is how far
"reach main" reaches: the tip tree (a required <code>backlog-ids</code> check, merge method unchanged) or full
reachability (squash-only).</p>

<p>A first version of the default, numbering at the drain's admission, was refuted on the repo's own code.
<code>pr-land</code> labels a PR <code>ready-to-merge</code> only after its required checks are green, and the
drain admits only labelled PRs. A required hash-free check is red on every fresh card-bearing PR, so none would ever
be admitted; and a drain-authored commit on a reviewed PR voids its approval (#3735). The old scope fork is a
forced invariant, the old collision fork follows the allocation choice, and the direct-push residual is #3423's
enforcement-rung knob, stated as a concession rather than a fork. The operator's integration-branch idea is kept as
Fork 1's alternative, with its real strength (testing the numbered combination of several PRs before
<code>main</code> moves) and its costs named.</p>

